<span class="kicker">2026-07-24 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Lay a Paver Walkway</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>Laying a paver walkway well comes down to two things: choosing a pattern that suits how the space will be used, and keeping your alignment and joint spacing consistent from the first paver to the last. Get those two right, and everything else, cutting, edging, sweeping in sand, falls into place. Get them wrong, and even a properly built base underneath won’t save the finished look.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, we lay paver walkways across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, and pattern choice is one of the first conversations we have with homeowners. This guide walks through how to actually lay the pavers once your base is ready, along with the patterns, materials, and techniques that separate a clean, lasting result from one that looks slightly off the moment it’s finished.</p>
<h2><b>Choosing a Paver Laying Pattern First</b></h2>
<p>Before placing a single paver, it helps to know which pattern you’re aiming for. The pattern affects how much cutting you’ll do, how the walkway holds up over time, and the overall look of the finished path.</p>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Pattern</b></td><td><b>Difficulty</b></td><td><b>Durability</b></td><td><b>Best For</b></td></tr><tr><td>Running Bond</td><td>Easy</td><td>Good</td><td>Straight, simple walkways</td></tr><tr><td>Herringbone</td><td>Moderate to hard</td><td>Excellent</td><td>High-traffic paths, driveway adjacent walkways</td></tr><tr><td>Basketweave</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Good</td><td>Traditional or formal looks</td></tr><tr><td>Stack Bond</td><td>Easy</td><td>Fair</td><td>Modern, minimalist designs</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Running bond</b> lays pavers in offset rows, similar to a brick wall pattern. It’s forgiving on curves and narrow sections, which makes it a common choice for a first DIY project. The tradeoff is that it relies more heavily on edge restraints to keep pavers from creeping sideways over time, since there’s no interlocking geometry doing that work for you.</p>
<p><b>Herringbone</b> places pavers at a 45 or 90 degree angle to each other, creating a zigzag look that locks each paver against its neighbors in two directions at once. This is generally considered the strongest pattern for load bearing and high traffic areas, which is why it shows up often on walkways that see regular foot traffic, wheelbarrows, or occasional vehicle overflow from an adjacent driveway.</p>
<p><b>Basketweave</b> uses pairs of pavers set at alternating angles to create a woven look. It reads as more traditional or formal, and works well for walkways leading up to a front entry where the overall design leans classic rather than modern.</p>
<p><b>Stack bond</b> lines pavers up in a straight grid, with no offset between rows. It gives a clean, minimalist look that pairs well with modern home exteriors, but it is the least forgiving pattern when it comes to hiding small measurement errors, since every joint lines up in both directions and any drift becomes obvious immediately.</p>
<p>If your walkway curves or narrows along its length, running bond is usually the most forgiving choice to work with. If it runs in a long, straight, high traffic line, herringbone tends to hold up the best over the years.</p>
<h2><b>What You Will Need Before You Start Laying Pavers</b></h2>
<p><b>Materials</b></p>
<ul><li>Pavers in your chosen pattern and quantity, plus 5 to 10 percent extra for cuts</li><li>Paver edge restraints and spikes</li><li>Polymeric sand</li><li>Coarse bedding sand</li></ul>
<p><b>Tools</b></p>
<ul><li>Rubber mallet</li><li>String line and stakes for keeping rows straight</li><li>A<a href="/blog/choose-the-right-plate-compactor-size-for-a-patio-or-driveway/"> properly sized plate compactor</a></li><li>4 foot level</li><li>Paver saw or angle grinder with a masonry blade</li><li>Push broom and knee pads</li></ul>
<h2><b>A Quick Note on Base Preparation</b></h2>
<p>Everything in this guide assumes your base and bedding sand are already in place. Base depth and compaction matter more than the laying technique itself, since even a perfectly laid pattern will shift if the ground underneath isn’t ready for it. If you haven’t gotten to that stage yet, our<a href="/blog/paver-base-depth-for-patios-proper-installation-tips/"> guide to proper paver base depth</a> covers exactly what’s needed before you start placing pavers.</p>
<h2><b>Choosing the Right Paver Material for Your Pattern</b></h2>
<p>The material you choose affects how easy or difficult certain patterns are to lay cleanly.</p>
<ul><li><b>Concrete pavers</b> are the most common choice for DIY projects because they come in consistent, uniform sizes, which makes complex patterns like herringbone much easier to line up correctly.</li><li><b>Natural stone, like flagstone,</b> has irregular shapes and thicknesses, which makes strict geometric patterns harder to achieve. It works better with a more organic, fitted layout rather than a repeating pattern.</li><li><b>Clay brick pavers</b> are similar in consistency to concrete pavers but tend to be smaller, which means more individual pieces and more cuts for the same square footage, especially with herringbone or basketweave.</li></ul>
<p>If this is your first time laying a pattern like herringbone, concrete pavers give the most predictable, beginner friendly results.</p>
<h2><b>Best Time of Year to Lay Pavers in Colorado</b></h2>
<p>Late spring through early fall is generally the best window for laying pavers along the Front Range, once the ground has fully thawed and before the first hard freeze returns. Laying pavers on partially frozen or waterlogged ground makes it difficult to achieve a stable, level bedding layer, and any settling that happens before winter tends to get worse once freeze and thaw cycles begin. If you’re working within a tight timeline, aim to finish the joint sand and final compaction at least a few weeks before the first expected frost, so everything has time to set properly.</p>

